<BaseLayout title="The Hedgehog Who Went to Key West — Huanyu Chen"
  description="SIGGRAPH 2024 in Denver, then a two-week road trip: New York, New England, the Great Lakes, Chicago, Orlando and Key West.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · July–August 2024</p>
  <h1>The Hedgehog Who Went to Key West</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">SIGGRAPH 2024 in Denver, then New York to Key West, a city a day.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>18 days · July 28 – August 14, 2024</strong><br />
    Los Angeles → Denver (SIGGRAPH 2024) → New York → New England → the Great Lakes → Chicago →
    Orlando → Key West → Los Angeles<br />
    2,541 miles driven · 13 states by car, plus Colorado and Georgia · 13 nights on the road, only two places twice ·
    nine university campuses, mostly unplanned · one toy hedgehog as mascot
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-overview.png'}><img src={P + 'map-overview.png'} alt="Map of the whole trip: Los Angeles to Denver to New York, a driving loop through New England, the Great Lakes and Chicago, flights to Orlando, a drive to Key West, and flights home via Atlanta." /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole loop. Solid lines are driving; dashed lines are flights.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    It started with a conference. SIGGRAPH 2024 was in Denver, and when it ended I didn't fly home. I flew to New
    York, where the other half of this story's "we" was already waiting. We picked up a rental car at JFK on the evening
    of August 1st and gave ourselves two weeks. We didn't set out to
    make it a race. But when the goal is the southernmost point of the continental United States and the starting line
    is New York, you end up sleeping in a different bed almost every night. Thirteen nights, and only twice did we get
    to unpack for a second day. The rest was a city a day: coffee in one state, dinner in another.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything below is reconstructed from the camera roll. A phone remembers where and when every photo was taken,
    and lined up in order, a few hundred pictures turn out to be a surprisingly honest diary. They record the
    detours we forgot, the hour we actually left a place, and how much of every day was spent looking up at buildings.
  </p>

  <h2>Prologue: SIGGRAPH in Denver</h2>
  <p>
    The trip began, as many graphics people's summers do, at <strong>SIGGRAPH</strong>. I left Los Angeles, alone, on the
    morning of July 28th, banking out over Santa Monica Bay with the whole coastline and the runways of LAX laid out
    below before the plane turned east. The first souvenir of the trip was bought in Denver that same afternoon: a
    Colorado fridge magnet, all elk, mountains and wildflowers, which turned out to be the first of many.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lax-takeoff.jpg'} alt="Aerial view of the Los Angeles coastline and LAX runways from a departing plane." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Leaving LA: the coast and LAX from above, July 28th.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'colorado-magnet.jpg'} alt="A Colorado souvenir magnet with mountains, elk and wildflowers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Magnet number one.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The conference opened that evening in a darkened hall the size of an aircraft hangar, thousands of people facing a
    wall of screens. Four days at the <strong>Colorado Convention Center</strong> followed: talks, the exhibition floor, too many
    conversations to count, and a forty-foot blue bear peering in through the glass at all of it. The bear,
    officially <em>I See What You Mean</em>, is the building's unofficial mascot. From the outside he presses his
    nose against the windows; from the inside he looms behind the giant SIGGRAPH letters in the lobby, which makes him
    the most photographed attendee of the week.
  </p>
  <p>
    The upper concourse has its own view: west across the city to the faint line of the Front Range, with the
    tall white figures of Jonathan Borofsky's <em>Dancers</em> spinning on a lawn below. On the show floor,
    studios and labs set up booths, and research groups printed banners announcing how many papers they had gotten into
    the conference, the academic equivalent of a sports team hanging championship pennants. Next year's
    conference was already advertising itself on the show floor: SIGGRAPH 2025, Vancouver.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'siggraph-keynote.jpg'} alt="A packed, dark conference hall with large screens at SIGGRAPH 2024." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>SIGGRAPH 2024, opening evening.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-blue-bear.jpg'} alt="The giant blue bear sculpture leaning against the glass wall of the Colorado Convention Center." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>I See What You Mean</em>, the Big Blue Bear.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'siggraph-letters.jpg'} alt="Large SIGGRAPH letters inside the convention center, with the blue bear visible through the window." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same bear, from the inside.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'siggraph-2025-teaser.jpg'} alt="A booth on the exhibition floor advertising SIGGRAPH 2025 in Vancouver, August 10 to 14." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Already selling next summer.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Conference food is its own genre. Lunches were whatever was fastest along the 16th Street Mall: a bento box one
    day, a poke bowl the next, a bowl of ramen on the third, with bubble tea as a recurring character. One
    evening I went out to the southern suburbs for Chinese food, fortune cookies included; another ended at an Indian
    restaurant a few blocks from the convention center.
  </p>
  <p>
    The best evening was a walk. Just after eight, as the light went orange, I headed south out of downtown: past the
    curved glass front of the Denver Justice Center, the U.S. Mint, and the columns of the old Carnegie library,
    now the McNichols Building. Then into <strong>Civic Center Park</strong>, with its flower beds, its curved Greek
    colonnade and an outdoor amphitheater, sitting between the gold-domed <strong>Colorado State Capitol</strong> at one
    end and the <strong>City and County Building</strong> at the other. The sunset lit the clouds behind the City and
    County Building's clock tower like a stage set.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'colorado-capitol.jpg'} alt="The Colorado State Capitol with its gold dome across Civic Center Park at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Colorado State Capitol from Civic Center Park.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-city-county.jpg'} alt="Denver's City and County Building under orange sunset clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The City and County Building at sunset.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    South of the park is Denver's cultural district, and it is a sculpture garden that happens to have buildings in it.
    The Central Library is a stack of colored postmodern blocks, with <em>The Yearling</em>, a painted pony standing on a
    giant red chair, out front. Across the plaza are a field of red columns and a huge dark
    curl of steel. I ended at the <strong>Denver Art Museum</strong>, whose Hamilton Building looks like a titanium
    shard trying to take off. Its outdoor sculptures include a giant dustpan and broom and a bronze cow lying in the
    grass with her calf. Even a hotel lobby next door turned out to be a small gallery: a horse built from what looks
    like driftwood, a bronze girl holding a bouquet, and a tabletop model of that same dustpan and broom.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-art-museum.jpg'} alt="The angular titanium-clad Hamilton Building of the Denver Art Museum at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Denver Art Museum's Hamilton Building.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-big-sweep.jpg'} alt="A giant blue dustpan and broom sculpture outside the Denver Art Museum." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>The Big Sweep</em>, outside the museum.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-yearling.jpg'} alt="A sculpture of a pinto pony standing on an enormous red chair outside the Denver Central Library." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>The Yearling</em>, outside the Denver Central Library.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On August 1st the conference wound down, and I caught a flight east to New York, where the road trip, and the
    "we", began. Denver International was packed with people heading home. The flight crossed the Plains over a
    patchwork of green circles, center-pivot irrigation seen from cruising altitude, and by evening a white Hyundai sedan
    was waiting for us in a parking lot at JFK. It would carry us a little over two thousand miles in nine days.
  </p>

  <h2>Act I: New York to Chicago, the long way</h2>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-northeast.png'}><img src={P + 'map-northeast.png'} alt="Detailed map of the drive from New York through New England, upstate New York, Pittsburgh, Ohio and Michigan to Chicago and Wisconsin, with numbered overnight stops." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Act I in detail: 2,147 miles and nine nights. Nx marks the night number.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The first few days set the rhythm. In <strong>New Haven</strong>, at midday, we walked past Yale's Sterling Library and
    the tower of the Humanities Quadrangle. Yale's Collegiate Gothic is so thorough that the stone looks centuries older
    than it is, and a twisted white sculpture on Broadway was the only thing that looked like it belonged to this
    century. By late afternoon we were in <strong>Providence</strong>, climbing College Hill for a walk on Brown's
    campus: through Faunce Arch onto the Main Green, past University Hall and Sayles Hall, and over to the bronze Brown
    Bear, standing on his hind legs on a granite plinth.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then came two nights outside <strong>Boston</strong>, our first real breather. That full day started with a drive
    through MIT in Cambridge, with the Boston skyline lined up across the river, and became the Freedom Trail. Boston
    Common came first, where the squirrels were extremely tame: one sprawled on a granite curb like a cat on a warm day
    and watched us photograph it. Then the gold-domed State House, King's Chapel, the Golden Teapot (a copper kettle
    hanging over a street corner since the 1870s), the brutalist bulk of City Hall, the Old State House dwarfed by
    office towers, and Faneuil Hall with Samuel Adams standing guard out front.
  </p>
  <p>
    By five o'clock we were in the North End, where Hanover Street was as crowded as you'd expect on a summer Saturday,
    and the trail finished, as it does, across the river in Charlestown at the <strong>Bunker Hill Monument</strong>, a
    granite obelisk we reached at six in the evening. At its foot, the trail's last sign reads "The Revolution Begins
    Here", which feels like a strange thing to read at the end of it.
  </p>

  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'boston-state-house.jpg'} alt="The Massachusetts State House with its gold dome." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Boston: the State House on Beacon Hill, along the Freedom Trail.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'boston-squirrel.jpg'} alt="A gray squirrel resting on a granite curb on Boston Common, looking at the camera." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Boston Common local.</figcaption></figure>

  <p>
    Then the arc bent west. At the Vermont welcome center just outside <strong>Brattleboro</strong>, a timber building
    with a giant wall map of the state, we made the most consequential purchase of the trip. In a glass case of local
    souvenirs sat a small toy hedgehog. (More on him later. There is a lot of him later.) From there it was one of those days
    that looks unreasonable written down: <strong>Brattleboro → Albany → Syracuse → Rochester</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Albany</strong> was the surprise. Empire State Plaza is a 1960s fever dream in marble: a row of identical
    towers, a long reflecting pool, a rusted steel sculpture of interlocking cubes, and The Egg, a performing arts
    center shaped like a flying saucer balanced on its edge. At one end, the turreted New York State Capitol looks like
    it was airlifted in from the Loire Valley, with a giant "I ❤ NY" sign in front of it for anyone who missed the
    point. From the plaza's edge you can look down over the highway interchange to the Hudson. The hedgehog's first
    official outing was a café just north of the city, where he sat for his portrait next to a pineapple slush.
  </p>

  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'albany-empire-plaza.jpg'} alt="I love NY sign in front of the New York State Capitol at Empire State Plaza." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Albany: Empire State Plaza.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hedgehog-albany.jpg'} alt="A small toy hedgehog next to a pineapple slush on a café table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The hedgehog's first day on the job, at a café in Loudonville, just north of Albany.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <p>
    The day ended with a golden-hour walk across <strong>Syracuse University</strong>'s hill. In twenty minutes
    we passed the domed Hendricks Chapel, the red-brick turrets of the Tolley Building, the stone Hall of Languages, the
    Gothic bulk of Crouse College, and the little Holden Observatory. Every one of them was lit warm and low by the
    setting sun, with the city spread out below. Downtown, the Soldiers' and Sailors' Monument was the last thing we
    saw before the night's drive to Rochester.
  </p>
  <p>
    The next morning started on the sand at Rochester's <strong>Ontario Beach Park</strong>: a boardwalk, a red-brick
    pavilion, and a wide beach with almost nobody on it, Lake Ontario flat and pale to the horizon. Then came the first
    jaw-drop of the trip: <strong>Niagara Falls</strong>, by way of <strong>Old Fort Niagara</strong> where the Niagara
    River meets Lake Ontario.
  </p>
  <p>
    We walked Goat Island out to Terrapin Point, close enough to the Horseshoe Falls to get wet. The mist rises so high
    that the Canadian skyline on the far bank comes and goes behind it, and far below, a tour boat full of people in
    ponchos nosed into the spray. From the other side of the island, the American Falls and the Rainbow Bridge line up
    with the Skylon Tower in Canada beyond, a view that looks exactly like a postcard because it is on most of them. On
    the way out, we said hello to the statue of Nikola Tesla, seated and reading, who helped harness the falls for
    electricity.
  </p>

  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'niagara-falls.jpg'} alt="Niagara Falls seen from the American side with the Rainbow Bridge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The American Falls, from Niagara Falls State Park.</figcaption></figure>

  <p>
    Then <strong>Buffalo</strong>, for wings at the Anchor Bar, which is credited with inventing them: a platter of
    them, with celery sticks and blue cheese. A sign painted on a building on Main Street asks visitors to "Keep Buffalo
    a Secret". The sky turned gray and rainy as we left. By the time we reached the beach at Presque Isle in
    <strong>Erie</strong>, it had cleared enough to look back across the bay at the city. Then came a long run south on
    I-79 toward Pittsburgh while the sky put on a show: towering anvil clouds lit gold at sunset, photographed through
    the windshield again and again because each one looked better than the last.
  </p>

  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'i79-sunset.jpg'} alt="Sunset-lit clouds over an interstate highway in Pennsylvania." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>I-79 in Crawford County, Pennsylvania, around 8 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <p>
    The Rust Belt was its own chapter. In <strong>Pittsburgh</strong> we went up Mount Washington, to Grandview
    Avenue and the top of the Monongahela Incline, for the view of the three rivers. Downtown sits on its wedge of land
    where the Allegheny and the Monongahela meet to form the Ohio, stitched to everything else by bridges. The incline's
    upper station has a bay window where people line up just to stand and look. Then we crossed town to Oakland. The
    <strong>Cathedral of Learning</strong> at Pitt is a forty-two-story Gothic skyscraper, a university building that
    looks like a church that kept growing. Next to it, Heinz Chapel's spire was wrapped in scaffolding. Across the street
    stand the Carnegie Museum and Music Hall and the Soldiers and Sailors Memorial, and a few blocks further on is
    Carnegie Mellon's campus. The afternoon ended with a matcha break for the hedgehog in Squirrel Hill.
  </p>

  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pittsburgh.jpg'} alt="Downtown Pittsburgh skyline and rivers from a hillside overlook." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pittsburgh from Grandview Avenue on Mount Washington.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hedgehog-pittsburgh.jpg'} alt="The toy hedgehog sitting between two matcha desserts." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Matcha break in Squirrel Hill.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <p>
    Then came a quick hop to <strong>Wheeling, West Virginia</strong>, whose downtown is a block of handsome Victorian
    brick that looks like it was built for a city three times the size. By dusk we were in
    <strong>Columbus</strong>, where we walked past the Ohio Statehouse (a capitol with a flat-topped drum where the
    dome should be, and William McKinley out front) under a heavy gray sky. Then a stroll up Neil Avenue onto the Ohio
    State campus as the lights came on, and dinner at the Hofbräuhaus: sausages, sauerkraut, mashed potatoes and a pork
    knuckle, about as far from Denver ramen as a week can get. After that came a night drive to just outside
    <strong>Cleveland</strong>. Which brings us back to the hedgehog.
  </p>

  <p>
    We checked out the next morning and walked around Case Western Reserve, past a white abstract sculpture on the lawn
    and Frank Gehry's business-school building, whose brick walls bulge and lean while sheets of steel pour off the roof
    like a building that is melting from the top down. We were well down the road when we realized the hedgehog wasn't
    with us. There was a brief, silent negotiation, and then we turned around, drove back, retrieved one (1) toy
    hedgehog, and carried on as if nothing had happened. The photo timestamps don't lie: we were in Cleveland until
    three in the afternoon. We spent the extra time on the lakefront at North Coast Harbor, where the "Cleveland"
    script sign sits in front of the white geometry of the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame, under a sky that was clearly
    thinking about a storm.
  </p>

  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cleveland.jpg'} alt="The Cleveland script sign on the lakefront with the downtown skyline." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cleveland's North Coast Harbor, where we stayed longer than planned.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'detroit.jpg'} alt="The Renaissance Center towers in downtown Detroit." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Detroit: the Renaissance Center, from Randolph Street.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By the time we reached <strong>Detroit</strong> the storm had blown off and the sky was a clean, hard blue. From
    the riverfront you look straight across the water at Canada, at the skyline of Windsor, Ontario, which from
    Detroit is due <em>south</em>. Downtown is dominated by the Renaissance Center, a cluster of dark glass cylinders
    that is very much a 1970s idea of the future. At Hart Plaza, by the river, stands <em>Transcending</em>, a giant
    steel ring broken at the top that honors the labor movement. The ring is left open on purpose, because the work is
    unfinished.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'detroit-transcending.jpg'} alt="Transcending, a tall broken steel ring honoring labor, at Hart Plaza in Detroit, with flags behind it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>Transcending</em>, the Michigan Labor Legacy Landmark at Hart Plaza, Detroit.</figcaption></figure>

  <p>
    From there the Great Lakes reeled us in. In <strong>Lansing</strong> at midday, the Michigan State Capitol looked
    small and perfect under a big sky streaked with cirrus. Then came a stop in <strong>Michigan City</strong>, Indiana,
    where Franklin Street is lined with painted metal sculptures and a red-brick church steeple.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lansing.jpg'} alt="The Michigan State Capitol in Lansing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lansing: the Michigan State Capitol.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'madison.jpg'} alt="The Wisconsin State Capitol in Madison under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Madison: the Wisconsin State Capitol, from Mansion Hill.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And then came <strong>Chicago</strong>, which we liked so much that we photographed it in three separate
    sessions: arriving in the late afternoon, a night on the way back, and a whole morning downtown. We came in up South
    Michigan Avenue, with the skyline building ahead of us block by block, and spent the first evening on foot. The
    Magnificent Mile has the old Water Tower, which survived the Great Fire of 1871, sitting like a sandcastle among the
    skyscrapers, and the tapering black X-braced Hancock tower. It also has a 24-hour "bakery ATM" that vends cakes,
    and, all along the avenue, giant guitar and banjo sculptures under banners reading "Chicago Welcomes DNC 2024" (the
    Democratic National Convention was eleven days away).
  </p>
  <p>
    At the river, the Wrigley Building and Tribune Tower face each other across Michigan Avenue like two wedding cakes.
    Below them, the river was a traffic jam of architecture-tour boats, each packed with people looking up. We followed
    the river east along Wacker Drive, past the glass tower that steps back in tiers and the wavy blue-glass St. Regis,
    and then went up the Hancock to the <strong>360 Chicago</strong> deck. Its museum floor glows with neon maps of the
    city's neighborhoods, and one window carries the slogan "My Block, My Hood, My City". We watched dusk fall from the
    top: the lakeshore curving north in a perfect arc, Navy Pier poking out into the lake, and the grid of the city
    switching on, light by light, all the way to the horizon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-river.jpg'} alt="The Chicago River lined with skyscrapers, with a tour boat below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Chicago River from Streeterville, first evening.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-from-above.jpg'} alt="Chicago's lakefront and Lake Shore Drive at dusk from an observation deck." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The lakefront at dusk, from 360 Chicago.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'navy-pier.jpg'} alt="The Navy Pier Ferris wheel against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Navy Pier, last morning.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That night we slept just over the Wisconsin line in Kenosha. The next day we looped north to
    <strong>Milwaukee</strong>, where a streetcar runs downtown, and on to <strong>Madison</strong>, whose capitol is
    a white granite dome at the top of the isthmus, with every street seeming to aim at it. Then back to Chicago in time
    to see a Gothic chapel lit up at dusk on the University of Chicago campus, its stone glowing gold from floodlights
    in the trees, and to sleep at Navy Pier. The window that night looked back across the harbor at the whole lit-up
    skyline. In the morning, the same window showed white cruise boats tied up below and the lake turned a ridiculous
    shade of blue.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-skyline.jpg'} alt="The Chicago skyline across Lake Michigan on a clear morning." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The skyline from the lake, the morning we left.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The last morning was the best weather of the trip. We walked from the pier back through Streeterville and down to
    the river, where the boats were already lining up, and spent an hour at the <strong>Chicago Architecture
    Center</strong>. Its scale model of downtown, with a film playing on a big screen behind it, is worth the trip on its own. Upstairs,
    a gallery of skyscraper portraits included a nod to home, Los Angeles's Eastern Columbia Building. There was also a
    black-and-gold model of the Carbide &amp; Carbon Building, and a row of white models of the world's tallest towers
    arranged in front of a window that looks straight out at the real Wrigley Building.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then came a ride on the <strong>Centennial Wheel</strong> at Navy Pier: the carousel and the pier's long roofs
    directly below, Lake Point Tower to one side, and the whole skyline in a single frame. And finally, for no good
    reason, we went to the front door of McDonald's <strong>Hamburger University</strong> in the West Loop, the
    company's training center, which really is called that. If the trip had a capital, it was Chicago.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-dnc-banjo.jpg'} alt="A giant banjo sculpture on Michigan Avenue with Chicago Welcomes DNC 2024 banners." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Michigan Avenue, eleven days before the convention.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'centennial-wheel-view.jpg'} alt="View down onto Navy Pier, its carousel and the Chicago skyline from the top of the Centennial Wheel." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Navy Pier from the top of the Centennial Wheel.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-city-model.jpg'} alt="A large scale model of downtown Chicago at the Chicago Architecture Center." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The city model at the Chicago Architecture Center.</figcaption></figure>


  <h2>Act II: The teleport</h2>
  <p>
    Here's where we cheated, and I'd do it again. The middle of the country is vast, our vacation days were not, and we
    wanted to end somewhere warm and improbable. So we turned in the car and flew from <strong>Chicago to
    Orlando</strong>, connecting through Atlanta without leaving the terminal, and stayed two nights in Orlando, doing
    the exact opposite of a road trip: standing in one place, riding things, and having a wonderful time.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first day was <strong>Universal Studios</strong>. Optimus Prime towers over the entrance to Transformers: The
    Ride 3D, which had a twenty-minute wait, practically nothing. The Simpsons' Springfield came next: Jebediah
    Springfield's statue, Chief Wiggum holding a donut beside his squad car, the Lard Lad Donuts statue, and a wall-sized
    mural of what seemed like every character the show has ever had. In the afternoon came the classic Florida summer
    rain shower, the kind that empties the paths in thirty seconds and is gone in twenty minutes. Then DreamWorks Land
    and the E.T. Adventure, under a sky that couldn't decide.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second day was <strong>SeaWorld</strong>, by way of the Orlando Eye on International Drive in the morning, its
    big white wheel framed by palm trees against storm clouds building up for the afternoon.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'universal-transformers.jpg'} alt="A giant Optimus Prime figure above the entrance to Transformers: The Ride 3D at Universal Studios Florida." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Universal Studios: Transformers, twenty-minute wait.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'orlando-eye.jpg'} alt="The Orlando Eye Ferris wheel framed by palm trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Orlando Eye on International Drive, the morning of the SeaWorld day.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Act III: The end of the road</h2>
  <p>
    After SeaWorld we drove south into the humidity and spent the night near <strong>Fort Lauderdale</strong>. The
    next morning took us through <strong>Miami Beach</strong> and across the causeway to downtown
    <strong>Miami</strong>, where a statue of Simón Bolívar stands in the heat. Then we went out onto the
    <strong>Overseas Highway</strong>, the ribbon of US-1 that island-hops across the ocean through Key Largo to
    <strong>Key West</strong>. For long stretches there is water on both sides and nothing else: the Gulf on the
    right, the Atlantic on the left, both the same unreal turquoise.
  </p>
  <p>
    We stopped for croquetas on Stock Island just before the last bridge and reached the end of the road at six in the
    evening. We had started near the top of the East Coast and driven, more or less, to the bottom of it. There's a
    buoy there, striped red, black and yellow and painted "90 miles to Cuba", that marks the southernmost point of the
    continental U.S. We took the photo. Everyone takes the photo. Then we went on to Mallory Square on the harbor, Key
    West's traditional sunset spot, where the flags were snapping in the wind and the sky over the Gulf was going gray
    and gold.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'southernmost-point.jpg'} alt="The Southernmost Point buoy in Key West: 90 miles to Cuba." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>90 miles to Cuba.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The last morning was a slow one. We drove along the beach on South Roosevelt Boulevard and then went to the
    <strong>Ernest Hemingway Home</strong>, which is, functionally, a cat sanctuary with a famous author's furniture in
    it. Dozens of cats live there, many of them six-toed descendants of a cat Hemingway was given. The cats sleep wherever
    they like, which is mostly on the exhibits: one was curled on Hemingway's own white bedspread behind a velvet rope,
    and another was asleep in a carved wooden chair whose sign politely asks visitors not to sit on the furniture. The
    sign is clearly meant for humans only.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'key-west-cat.jpg'} alt="A black cat asleep on an antique bed at the Hemingway House." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A resident of the Ernest Hemingway Home, off duty.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lobster-roll.jpg'} alt="A lobster roll in a takeout box." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Last lunch in Key West, at the airport.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <p>
    Getting home had one last surprise. We connected through Atlanta again, but this layover ran long enough to do
    something slightly unhinged: we rode <strong>MARTA</strong> into downtown Atlanta and wandered around Five Points.
    We passed the Henry W. Grady monument, downtown's towers and, from an overpass, the folded
    steel-and-glass petals of Mercedes-Benz Stadium, with construction cranes still working around it. The station
    entrance at Five Points is decorated with giant painted X-shaped sculptures, which is as good a way as any to mark a
    spot. Then we rode back in time for the flight home to <strong>Los Angeles</strong>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'marta.jpg'} alt="A MARTA station entrance in downtown Atlanta with colorful sculptures." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Five Points, downtown Atlanta, for about twenty minutes.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>The hedgehog, and the magnets</h2>
  <p>
    Every trip needs a mascot, and ours chose himself in Vermont. The hedgehog rode along for two weeks: he posed with a
    slush in Albany and a matcha sundae in Pittsburgh, was left behind in Cleveland and rescued, and made it all the way
    home. His story doesn't end there, which is the sad and funny part: months later he came with us to the San Diego
    Zoo, and this time we lost him for good. So we did the only reasonable thing. We bought another one, the exact same
    toy, and pretended nothing had happened.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other souvenir was quieter. At stop after stop we picked up a fridge magnet: cheap, tacky, perfect. They're all
    on our refrigerator now, a cluttered map of the whole loop that we walk past every morning. There's Vermont, the
    mitten of Michigan, the shape of Wisconsin, a cassette tape, a little family of Simpsons, and many more, in no
    particular order, which is how memory works anyway.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'fridge-magnets.jpg'} alt="A refrigerator door covered in souvenir magnets from the trip." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The scrapbook.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Would we do it again?</h2>
  <p>
    A city a day is not the way to know a place. It is the way to meet a lot of them. We got a handshake with two dozen
    towns and a real conversation with maybe three. If I did it again I'd trade a couple of the one-nighters for more
    time in Chicago, or on a porch in Vermont doing nothing at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    And without ever planning it, we toured nine campuses: Yale, Brown, MIT, Syracuse, Pitt, Carnegie Mellon, Ohio
    State, Case Western and the University of Chicago. Apparently a week at SIGGRAPH doesn't switch that part of the
    brain off. We also saw a remarkable number of state capitols (Colorado, Massachusetts, New York, Ohio, Michigan,
    Wisconsin) without meaning to, which says something about how American cities like to put their best building at
    the end of the main street.
  </p>
  <p>
    But there's something to be said for the blur, too. A trip like this is mostly the in-between: rain on the windshield in
    Buffalo, the anvil clouds on I-79, a rest stop that sells hedgehogs. Two weeks, one summer, the eastern half of the
    country out the windshield, and a toy hedgehog on the dashboard.
  </p>
